# Deliberately empty of a catch-all. Read this before adding one back.
#
# `/*  /index.html  200` was here to keep client-side routes alive across a hard
# reload. There are no client-side routes — `src/routes/+page.svelte` is the only
# one and it prerenders to `/index.html` — so it bought nothing, and it cost an
# outage:
#
#   1. During a deploy a browser asked for a hashed chunk before it had
#      propagated to that edge node.
#   2. No asset matched, so this rule answered with the SPA shell at status 200.
#   3. `_headers` marks `/_app/immutable/*` as `max-age=31536000, immutable`, so
#      Cloudflare cached that HTML **under the JavaScript URL, for a year**.
#   4. Every later request for the real chunk got HTML. `import()` rejects, and
#      the app dies with SvelteKit's opaque "500 Internal Error".
#
# Observed in production as `content-type: text/html` with `cf-cache-status: HIT`
# on a .js URL. Appending a query string returned the correct JavaScript, which
# is what proved it was the cache entry rather than the file.
#
# Cloudflare Pages `_redirects` supports 200 rewrites and 3xx redirects but NOT
# 404, so there is no way to keep a catch-all that fails safely for assets. With
# no rule, a missing asset gets a real 404: `import()` still fails, but it fails
# cleanly and nothing cacheable is ever served in its place.
#
# The trade-off, stated: a deep link to any path other than `/` now returns
# Cloudflare's 404 instead of loading the app. That is correct for a single-route
# app whose PWA `start_url` is `/`. If real routes are ever added, scope the
# rewrite to those paths explicitly — never `/*`, or this comes straight back.
